content:
Plan revised (HEAD of phase/cockpit-lanes-read-as-terminals). Both gaps closed; the nonblocking guidance is written into the approach. Part 4 (owner liveness) stays in this phase, as you ruled. No arbiter interjection; the three recommended UX choices proceed.

1. The watcher-off card. It no longer tells its own story: it keeps its trigger (headline.state == 'watcher-off') and its Start-the-watcher action, but its title/body are headline.text, so a delivered terminal turn reads 'its turn was sent; the watcher has since stopped, so the next hand-off will not happen' instead of 'Nothing runs X's turn until the watcher is on'. Delivery is never re-derived in JS. The stalled / watcher-stale card does the same ('…it has its turn; the watcher has stopped looking' never implies the agent is stuck). Tests: delivered and undelivered variants of both cards; no card says 'Nothing runs' for a delivered turn. Criterion 6 now covers watcher-off as well as stalled.

2. One bounded-stream policy, realizable with what exists (verified: MAX_TAIL_LINES = 2000; the tail payload is {path, lines, …} with NO truncation flag; appendActLine caps a cycle block at 2,000; LEAD.lines grows without a cap):
   - cap: newest 2,000 lines for every block — running or finished, cycle or chat; the chat stream gets the cap it lacks;
   - honest label instead of a 'whole log' link: a first-row note 'showing the last 2,000 lines — the whole log: <path>' when a running block dropped lines or a finished tail returns exactly the cap ('may be longer' — the only signal available), path from the tail payload / the item's log_path, as selectable text. No server change;
   - trim vs. anchor: trimmed from the top; while scrolled back the anchor is the first visible LINE (rowTopIn); if trimming removes it, the view rests on the oldest retained line, never jumps to the bottom, cue stays;
   - fold: detaches the listener, empties the stream, and late arrivals — an in-flight SSE line/end frame or a delayed /api/tail response — are DISCARDED for a folded block (block key + fold generation counter, checked at delivery);
   - reopen = one consistent read: finished → /api/tail?lines=2000, rendered once; running → re-attach from after=0 into an empty stream with the cap applied as lines arrive. No splicing of kept and arriving lines, so nothing is duplicated or omitted; cursors are kept for blocks that stay open and reset by a fold. Chat blocks follow the same rules through /api/lead/<cid>/events.
   New criterion 4a. Tests: caps for a running cycle block and a running chat block (2,050 in → 2,000 kept, note + path shown); fold drops stream DOM; a final line/end frame and a delayed tail after the fold change nothing; fold → open → fold → open renders the tail once each time; a running reopen starts at after=0; polling does not rebuild an open chat block's stream (node identity preserved across renderLeadTimeline).

Guidance taken: fillMsgRow's signature includes the streamed line count, so polling rebuilds the chat body today — the replacement keys the stream DOM and appends (signature = status/reply/error only); reply, error and the continuity note are kept (closing lines / header). Real Chromium now covers a cycle block AND a chat block: follow, hold, and cap rollover while scrolled back (anchor survives trimming; anchor trimmed → oldest retained line, cue shown), one scroller, no 16em box.

Docs only; nothing implemented; no tests run.
